PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L08U4.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":8,"cefr":"B2a","unit":4,"theme":"Persuasion and Advertising","essential_question":"How do advertisers try to persuade us, and how can we think for ourselves?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1","title":"I Needn't Have Rushed: Modals of Obligation, Necessity and Expectation","objectives":["Students will be able to express obligation, no obligation, prohibition and expectation with the right modal.","Students will be able to talk about past necessity with had to, didn't need to, needn't have done and was supposed to.","I can explain the difference between \"mustn't\" and \"don't have to\".","I can say \"I needn't have rushed\" and \"I was supposed to return it within seven days.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعبير عن الإلزام وعدم الإلزام والمنع والتوقع بالفعل المناسب.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التحدث عن الضرورة في الماضي بـ had to وdidn't need to وneedn't have done وwas supposed to.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين mustn't وdon't have to.","أستطيع أن أقول I needn't have rushed وI was supposed to return it within seven days."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"advertisement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ædˈvɝtəzmənt/","definition":"a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something","arabic":"إعلان","example":"I saw an advertisement for a new phone.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W01"},{"word":"consumer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsumɚ/","definition":"a person who buys goods or services","arabic":"مستهلك","example":"Consumers have the right to honest information.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W02"},{"word":"claim","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kleɪm/","definition":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء","example":"The ad made the claim that the cream works in one day.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W03"},{"word":"persuade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pɚˈsweɪd/","definition":"to make someone agree to do or believe something","arabic":"يُقنع","example":"The salesman persuaded my father to buy a bigger TV.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"must / have to / need to = it is necessary. must often shows the speaker's own rule; have to often shows an outside rule. mustn't = it is not allowed (prohibition). don't have to / don't need to / needn't = it is not necessary (you can choose). These are very different: You mustn't share your password. (Don't do it!) ↔ You don't have to pay now. (It's not necessary.)\nbe supposed to = something is expected because of a rule, an arrangement or what people say, but often it doesn't happen: Ads are supposed to be honest. In the past, was / were supposed to shows that an expected action didn't happen: I was supposed to return them within seven days (but I didn't).\nFor the past: had to (it was necessary); didn't have to / didn't need to (it wasn't necessary, so I probably didn't do it); needn't have + past participle (I did it, but it wasn't necessary): I needn't have rushed. (I rushed, but there was no need.)\nmust / have to / need to = necessary\nmustn't = prohibited • don't have to / needn't = not necessary\nbe supposed to = expected (often not done)\npast: had to • didn't need to (didn't do it) • needn't have done (did it – unnecessary)","examples":["You must keep the receipt if you want a refund.","Students don't have to wear uniforms on Thursday.","You mustn't believe every review you read online.","The new phone is supposed to be waterproof.","We had to wait two weeks for the delivery.","I needn't have paid for express delivery—it arrived late anyway.","The sale was supposed to end on Friday, but they extended it.","I didn't need to buy a case because one came with the phone."],"common_errors":["✗ You mustn't pay now; you can pay later. → ✓ You don't have to pay now; you can pay later. (Not necessary → don't have to.)","✗ I needn't have bought a charger, so I didn't. → ✓ I didn't need to buy a charger, so I didn't. (Didn't do it → didn't need to.)","✗ We must to read the terms. → ✓ We must read the terms. (No to after must.)","✗ The parcel supposed to arrive yesterday. → ✓ The parcel was supposed to arrive yesterday. (be + supposed to.)"],"arabic":"must / have to / need to للضرورة، وmustn't للمنع، وdon't have to / needn't لعدم الضرورة: You mustn't share your password ≠ You don't have to pay now.\nbe supposed to للتوقّع بحسب قاعدة أو اتفاق، وكثيرًا لا يحدث: The parcel was supposed to arrive yesterday (كان من المفترض أن يصل).\nفي الماضي: had to (كان ضروريًّا)، didn't need to (لم يكن ضروريًّا فلم أفعله)، needn't have done (فعلته ولم يكن ضروريًّا).\nخطأ شائع عند العرب: الخلط بين mustn't وdon't have to لأن «لا يجب» في العربية تُفهم بالمعنيين."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Needn't Have Rushed: Modals of Obligation, Necessity and Expectation”. Let’s learn must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U4-LS1 | I Needn't Have Rushed: Modals of Obligation, Necessity and Expectation","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Don't Believe Everything You See”: Last month, I saw an online advertisement for a pair of wireless headphones. \"Limited offer! Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Last month, I saw an online advertisement for a pair of wireless headphones. \"Limited offer!","visual":"Image GV-L08-U4-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"must / have to / need to = it is necessary. must often shows the speaker's own rule; have to often shows an outside rule. mustn't = it is not allowed (prohibition). don't have to / don't need to / needn't = it is not necessary (you can choose). These are very different: You mustn't share your password. (Don't do it!) ↔ You don't have to pay now. (It's not necessary.) be supposed to = something is expected because of a rule, an arrangement or what people say, but often it doesn't happen: Ads are supposed to be honest. In the past, was / were supposed to shows that an expected action didn't happen: I was supposed to return them within seven days (but I didn't).","on_screen":"must / have to / need to = necessary • mustn't = prohibited • don't have to / needn't = not necessary • be supposed to = expected (often not done)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. You must keep the receipt if you want a refund. Students don't have to wear uniforms on Thursday. You mustn't believe every review you read online.","on_screen":"You must keep the receipt if you want a refund. / Students don't have to wear uniforms on Thursday. / You mustn't believe every review you read online.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “You mustn't pay now; you can pay later.” That’s not right. We say: “You don't have to pay now; you can pay later.” Not necessary → don't have to.","on_screen":"✗ You mustn't pay now; you can pay later.   ✓ You don't have to pay now; you can pay later.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"You mustn't open the box.\" Can I open it? … Think! … No – it's not allowed. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"mustn't\" and \"don't have to\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain the difference between \"mustn't\" and \"don't have to\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","must / have to / need to للضرورة، وmustn't للمنع، وdon't have to / needn't لعدم الضرورة: You mustn't share your password ≠ You don't have to pay now.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: You mustn't pay now; you can pay later. — الصحيح: You don't have to pay now; you can pay later.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين mustn't وdon't have to."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Don't Believe Everything You See","body":"Last month, I saw an online advertisement for a pair of wireless headphones. \"Limited offer! Only 3 left! You need to order now!\" it said. The photos were amazing, and a famous gamer claimed they were \"the best headphones in the world.\" I didn't want to miss the chance, so I bought them immediately.\nWhen they arrived, I was disappointed. The sound was poor, and the battery lasted only two hours. I needn't have rushed: a week later, the same headphones were still on sale—at a lower price! I should have read the reviews first.\nMy father explained that in many countries, advertisers are not allowed to make false claims. Ads are supposed to be honest, and paid endorsements must be clearly labeled. \"But you have to be careful,\" he said. \"Not every website follows the rules.\"\nI wanted to return the headphones, but I was supposed to send them back within seven days, and I had waited too long. Luckily, the shop let me exchange them for a cheaper model, so I didn't have to pay anything extra.\nNow I have a new rule: before I buy anything online, I must compare prices and read at least five reviews. I don't need to buy something just because an ad says \"Hurry!\" Smart shoppers mustn't let advertisers make their decisions for them.","word_count":222,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"You ___ share your bank details with strangers online.","options":["don't have to","don't need to","needn't","mustn't"],"answer":"mustn't","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."},{"q":"Delivery is free, so you ___ pay anything extra.","options":["can't","don't have to","mustn't","aren't allowed to"],"answer":"don't have to","explanation":"Not necessary → don't have to / don't need to / needn't.","explanation_ar":"عدم الضرورة."},{"q":"I bought a new charger, but there was already one in the box. I ___ it.","options":["don't have to buy","mustn't buy","didn't need to buy","needn't have bought"],"answer":"needn't have bought","explanation":"Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"فعلته دون حاجة."},{"q":"The parcel ___ arrive yesterday, but it still hasn't come.","options":["must have to","had supposed","was supposed to","supposes to"],"answer":"was supposed to","explanation":"Expected → be supposed to (often not done).","explanation_ar":"التوقّع."},{"q":"We ___ wait two weeks for our order because the shop was very busy.","options":["must","have had","had to","needed wait"],"answer":"had to","explanation":"Past necessity → had to / didn't have to / didn't need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة في الماضي."},{"q":"In many countries, companies ______ to make false claims in their advertisements, and they can be fined if they do.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["are not allowed","not allowed","do not allowed","are not allow"],"answer":"are not allowed","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct form. Word bank: mustn't, don't have to, is supposed to","items":[{"q":"You ______ (prohibition) use your phone while driving.","options":[],"answer":"mustn't","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."},{"q":"Students ______ (no obligation) bring lunch; the school provides it.","options":[],"answer":"don't have to","explanation":"Not necessary → don't have to / don't need to / needn't.","explanation_ar":"عدم الضرورة."},{"q":"The new game ______ (expectation) be released next week.","options":[],"answer":"is supposed to","explanation":"Expected → be supposed to (often not done).","explanation_ar":"التوقّع."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"You mustn't come early; the shop opens at ten.","options":[],"answer":"You don't have to come early; the shop opens at ten.","explanation":"mustn't → don't have to. Not necessary → don't have to / don't need to / needn't.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: mustn't → don't have to. عدم الضرورة."},{"q":"I needn't have taken a taxi, so I walked.","options":[],"answer":"I didn't need to take a taxi, so I walked.","explanation":"needn't have taken → didn't need to take. Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: needn't have taken → didn't need to take. فعلته دون حاجة."},{"q":"We must to read the terms before we sign.","options":[],"answer":"We must read the terms before we sign.","explanation":"must to → must. Necessary → must / have to / need to.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: must to → must. الضرورة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Rules and regrets.","items":[{"q":"Something you have to do every school day: ___","options":[],"answer":"I have to wake up at 5:30 every school day.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you don't have to do on weekends: ___","options":[],"answer":"I don't have to wear my uniform on weekends.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you mustn't do in an exam: ___","options":[],"answer":"We mustn't talk to other students during an exam.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you needn't have done last week: ___","options":[],"answer":"I needn't have studied chapter four; it wasn't in the test.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something that was supposed to happen but didn't: ___","options":[],"answer":"The bus was supposed to arrive at seven, but it was late.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Smart shopper's rules.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 rules for shopping online with must, mustn't and don't have to.","options":[],"answer":"You must read the reviews. You mustn't click on suspicious links. You don't have to buy something just because it's on sale.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about an unnecessary purchase with needn't have.","options":[],"answer":"I needn't have bought a new bag; my old one was still fine.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"is supposed to\" about a product.","options":[],"answer":"This watch is supposed to last ten years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin & Speak","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Work in teams of four. Player 1 spins the wheel.","Make a correct sentence about the unit theme using the cue word and must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to.","The team checks the sentence with the grammar box. Correct = 1 point; an extra detail = 1 bonus point.","Pass the spinner clockwise. Play for 6 minutes; the team with most points wins."],"data":[{"label":"must"},{"label":"have to"},{"label":"don't have to"},{"label":"mustn't"},{"label":"needn't have…"},{"label":"was supposed to…"},{"label":"is advertised as…"},{"label":"has been tested"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Visitors ___ touch the paintings in the museum.","options":["don't need to","needn't","mustn't","don't have to"],"answer":"mustn't","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."},{"q":"You ___ print your ticket; you can show it on your phone.","options":["aren't allowed to","mustn't","don't have to","can't"],"answer":"don't have to","explanation":"Not necessary → don't have to / don't need to / needn't.","explanation_ar":"عدم الضرورة."},{"q":"I made a big cake, but only four guests came. I ___ so much.","options":["needn't have made","mustn't make","don't have to make","didn't need to make"],"answer":"needn't have made","explanation":"Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"فعلته دون حاجة."},{"q":"","options":["We are supposed meeting at six, but he forgot.","We supposed to meet at six, but he forgot.","We were supposed to meet at six, but he forgot.","We were suppose to meet at six, but he forgot."],"answer":"We were supposed to meet at six, but he forgot.","explanation":"Expected → be supposed to (often not done).","explanation_ar":"التوقّع."},{"q":"","options":["I needn't have buy a case because one came with the phone.","I didn't need to buy a case because one came with the phone.","I didn't needed to buy a case because one came with the phone.","I mustn't buy a case because one came with the phone."],"answer":"I didn't need to buy a case because one came with the phone.","explanation":"Past necessity → had to / didn't have to / didn't need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة في الماضي."},{"q":"","options":["Have we to registering before the event?","Must we to register before the event?","Do we must register before the event?","Do we have to register before the event?"],"answer":"Do we have to register before the event?","explanation":"Necessary → must / have to / need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة."},{"q":"According to the rules, every advertisement for a medicine ___ include a warning about side effects.","options":["needn't","doesn't have to","mustn't","must"],"answer":"must","explanation":"Necessary → must / have to / need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة."},{"q":"Children ______ to buy energy drinks in many countries because of the drinks' high caffeine content.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["are not allowed","not allowing","do not allowed","are not allow"],"answer":"are not allowed","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."},{"q":"The new model ______ last for ten hours on a single charge, but many customers say that the battery dies after only five.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["supposes to","is supposed to","supposed to be","is supposing"],"answer":"is supposed to","explanation":"Expected → be supposed to (often not done).","explanation_ar":"التوقّع."},{"q":"\"You needn't have paid for express delivery.\" What does this mean?","options":["You must pay now.","You paid, but it wasn't necessary.","You are not allowed to pay.","You didn't pay, which was right."],"answer":"You paid, but it wasn't necessary.","explanation":"Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"فعلته دون حاجة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"Saad at home opening a delivery box of shiny wireless headphones, a phone screen showing a flashy ad with \"ONLY 3 LEFT! HURRY!\", and a second panel with Saad and his father reading online reviews together. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Saad at home opening a delivery box of shiny wireless headphones, a phone screen showing a flashy ad with \"ONLY 3 LEFT! HURRY!\", and a second panel with Saad and his father reading online reviews together"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about must / have to / need to"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows advertisement (a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing advertisement: a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows consumer (a person who buys goods or services); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing consumer: a person who buys goods or services"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows claim (a statement that something is true, often without proof); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing claim: a statement that something is true, often without proof"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows persuade (to make someone agree to do or believe something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing persuade: to make someone agree to do or believe something"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin & Speak”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin & Speak"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W01","script":"advertisement. advertisement. I saw an advertisement for a new phone.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W02","script":"consumer. consumer. Consumers have the right to honest information.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W03","script":"claim. claim. The ad made the claim that the cream works in one day.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-W04","script":"persuade. persuade. The salesman persuaded my father to buy a bigger TV.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-T1","script":"Last month, I saw an online advertisement for a pair of wireless headphones. \"Limited offer! Only 3 left! You need to order now!\" it said. The photos were amazing, and a famous gamer claimed they were \"the best headphones in the world.\" I didn't want to miss the chance, so I bought them immediately. When they arrived, I was disappointed. The sound was poor, and the battery lasted only two hours. I needn't have rushed: a week later, the same headphones were still on sale—at a lower price! I should have read the reviews first. My father explained that in many countries, advertisers are not allowed to make false claims. Ads are supposed to be honest, and paid endorsements must be clearly labeled. \"But you have to be careful,\" he said. \"Not every website follows the rules.\" I wanted to return the headphones, but I was supposed to send them back within seven days, and I had waited too long. Luckily, the shop let me exchange them for a cheaper model, so I didn't have to pay anything extra. Now I have a new rule: before I buy anything online, I must compare prices and read at least five reviews. I don't need to buy something just because an ad says \"Hurry!\" Smart shoppers mustn't let advertisers make their decisions for them.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Needn't Have Rushed: Modals of Obligation, Necessity and Expectation”. Let’s learn must / have to / need to; don't have to / don't need to / needn't (no obligation); mustn't / not be allowed to (prohibition); be supposed to (expectation); past forms: had to, didn't have to / didn't need to, needn't have + past participle, was supposed to together. Listen to the start of “Don't Believe Everything You See”: Last month, I saw an online advertisement for a pair of wireless headphones. \"Limited offer! Look at the green words. must / have to / need to = it is necessary. must often shows the speaker's own rule; have to often shows an outside rule. mustn't = it is not allowed (prohibition). don't have to / don't need to / needn't = it is not necessary (you can choose). These are very different: You mustn't share your password. (Don't do it!) ↔ You don't have to pay now. (It's not necessary.) be supposed to = something is expected because of a rule, an arrangement or what people say, but often it doesn't happen: Ads are supposed to be honest. In the past, was / were supposed to shows that an expected action didn't happen: I was supposed to return them within seven days (but I didn't). Here are some examples. You must keep the receipt if you want a refund. Students don't have to wear uniforms on Thursday. You mustn't believe every review you read online. Be careful! Some learners say: “You mustn't pay now; you can pay later.” That’s not right. We say: “You don't have to pay now; you can pay later.” Not necessary → don't have to. Quick check: \"You mustn't open the box.\" Can I open it? … Think! … No – it's not allowed. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"mustn't\" and \"don't have to\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2","title":"\"Mistakes Were Made\": Active vs Passive for Effect","objectives":["Students will be able to explain why a writer chooses the active or passive voice.","Students will be able to rewrite sentences in the active or passive to achieve a particular effect.","I can explain why an ad says \"Clinically tested\" instead of \"We tested it.\"","I can choose the active voice to make a message clear and strong."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من شرح سبب اختيار الكاتب للمبني للمعلوم أو المجهول.","سيتمكن الطلاب من إعادة كتابة الجمل بالمعلوم أو المجهول لتحقيق أثر معيّن.","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا يقول الإعلان Clinically tested بدلًا من We tested it.","أستطيع أن أختار المبني للمعلوم لتكون الرسالة واضحة وقوية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"endorse","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛnˈdɔrs/","definition":"to say publicly that you support a product or a person","arabic":"يروّج لـ / يؤيّد","example":"A famous athlete endorses this brand of shoes.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W01"},{"word":"misleading","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɪˈslidɪŋ/","definition":"giving a wrong idea or impression","arabic":"مضلِّل","example":"The photo in the ad was misleading; the room was much smaller.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W02"},{"word":"guarantee","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɡɛrənˈti/","definition":"a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem","arabic":"ضمان","example":"The laptop comes with a two-year guarantee.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W03"},{"word":"genuine","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈdʒɛnjəwən/","definition":"real, not fake","arabic":"أصلي / حقيقي","example":"Is this a genuine leather bag?","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"The active voice says who does the action. It is clear, direct and energetic: We guarantee every product. Our team designed this app. Writers use it when they want to show responsibility or make a strong, personal message.\nThe passive voice focuses on the action or the result, not the doer: Your order has been shipped. Writers choose it when the agent is unknown, obvious or unimportant (The road was repaired last week), when they want to sound formal or scientific (The samples were heated to 50°C), or when they want to hide responsibility (Mistakes were made. – by whom?).\nAdvertisers often use the passive to sound impressive without giving details: \"Clinically tested\", \"Recommended by experts\" – Which experts? Tested how? Add by + agent only when the doer is important or new information: The bridge was designed by a Saudi engineer.\nactive = clear responsibility, direct, energetic\npassive = focus on the result / agent unknown or unimportant / formal / can hide the doer\nby + agent only when the doer matters\npassive = be + past participle (in any tense)","examples":["Your package has been delivered. (focus on the package)","Our engineers tested the phone in extreme heat. (credit to the engineers)","Penicillin was discovered in 1928.","The new law was passed last year.","We will refund your money within seven days. (clear promise)","The prices have been reduced by 30%.","The mosque was built in the 18th century.","I broke the vase, and I'm sorry. (honest apology)"],"common_errors":["✗ The cake was eaten by someone. (we know it was Omar) → ✓ Omar ate the cake. (Agent known and important → active.)","✗ The samples were heat to 50 degrees. → ✓ The samples were heated to 50 degrees. (be + past participle.)","✗ The bridge was designed by people. → ✓ The bridge was designed by a team of Saudi engineers. / The bridge was designed in 2015. (\"by people\" adds nothing – give a real agent or omit it.)","✗ Water is boiled at 100°C by it. → ✓ Water boils at 100°C. (Natural processes → active intransitive verb.)"],"arabic":"المبني للمعلوم يوضح مَن قام بالفعل، وهو مباشر وقوي ويُظهر المسؤولية: We guarantee every product.\nالمبني للمجهول يركّز على النتيجة، ويُستخدم عندما يكون الفاعل مجهولًا أو غير مهم، أو لإضفاء طابع رسمي وعلمي، أو لإخفاء المسؤولية: Mistakes were made.\nالمعلنون يستخدمون المجهول ليبدو كلامهم مقنعًا دون تفاصيل: Clinically tested — مَن اختبره؟\nنضيف by + الفاعل فقط عندما يكون مهمًّا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “\"Mistakes Were Made\": Active vs Passive for Effect”. Let’s learn choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U4-LS2 | \"Mistakes Were Made\": Active vs Passive for Effect","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Active: The company raised prices. • Passive: Prices were raised. Active: Nobody tested the cream. • Passive: The cream wasn't tested. Active: Who designed the logo? • Passive: Who was the logo designed by?","on_screen":"Active: The company raised prices. • Passive: Prices were raised. | Active: Nobody tested the cream. • Passive: The cream wasn't tested. | Active: Who designed the logo? • Passive: Who was the logo designed by?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"The active voice says who does the action. It is clear, direct and energetic: We guarantee every product. Our team designed this app. Writers use it when they want to show responsibility or make a strong, personal message. The passive voice focuses on the action or the result, not the doer: Your order has been shipped. Writers choose it when the agent is unknown, obvious or unimportant (The road was repaired last week), when they want to sound formal or scientific (The samples were heated to 50°C), or when they want to hide responsibility (Mistakes were made. – by whom?).","on_screen":"active = clear responsibility, direct, energetic • passive = focus on the result / agent unknown or unimportant / formal / can hide the doer • by + agent only when the doer matters","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Your package has been delivered. (focus on the package) Our engineers tested the phone in extreme heat. (credit to the engineers) Penicillin was discovered in 1928.","on_screen":"Your package has been delivered. (focus on the package) / Our engineers tested the phone in extreme heat. (credit to the engineers) / Penicillin was discovered in 1928.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The cake was eaten by someone. (we know it was Omar).” That’s not right. We say: “Omar ate the cake.” Agent known and important → active.","on_screen":"✗ The cake was eaten by someone. (we know it was Omar)   ✓ Omar ate the cake.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"Mistakes were made.\" Do we know who made the mistakes? … Think! … No – the passive hides the doer. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain why an ad says \"Clinically tested\" instead of \"We tested it.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain why an ad says \"Clinically tested\" instead of \"We tested it.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","المبني للمعلوم يوضح مَن قام بالفعل، وهو مباشر وقوي ويُظهر المسؤولية: We guarantee every product.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The cake was eaten by someone. (we know it was Omar) — الصحيح: Omar ate the cake.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا يقول الإعلان Clinically tested بدلًا من We tested it."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"A company wants to take clear responsibility for an error. Which sentence is best?","options":["We made a mistake, and we will fix it.","A mistake has been made by someone.","Mistakes were made.","Errors happened."],"answer":"We made a mistake, and we will fix it.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"Which sentence focuses on the result, not the doer?","options":["Our team shipped your order.","Your order has been shipped.","We shipped your order.","Ahmed shipped your order."],"answer":"Your order has been shipped.","explanation":"Passive voice to focus on the result.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"Why does the ad say \"Clinically tested\" instead of naming the tester?","options":["to make the sentence active","to make it a question","to show exactly who tested it","to sound impressive without giving details"],"answer":"to sound impressive without giving details","explanation":"Why did the writer choose this voice?","explanation_ar":"سبب الاختيار."},{"q":"","options":["The new stadium was designing by a famous architect.","The new stadium was design by a famous architect.","The new stadium was designed by a famous architect.","The new stadium designed by a famous architect."],"answer":"The new stadium was designed by a famous architect.","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"Which sentence uses \"by + agent\" usefully?","options":["The vaccine was developed by people.","The vaccine was developed by someone.","The vaccine was developed by them.","The vaccine was developed by a team at Oxford University."],"answer":"The vaccine was developed by a team at Oxford University.","explanation":"Include or omit by + agent.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل مع by."},{"q":"In a science report, the students wrote: \"The water ______ to 80°C, and its temperature was recorded every minute.\"\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["was heated","heated by","was heat","has heating"],"answer":"was heated","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite in the passive. Omit the agent if it isn't important.","items":[{"q":"Someone has stolen my bike.  (passive)","options":[],"answer":"My bike has been stolen.","explanation":"Passive voice to focus on the result.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"The company reduced the prices by 20%.  (passive)","options":[],"answer":"The prices were reduced by 20%.","explanation":"Passive voice to focus on the result.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"People speak Arabic in more than twenty countries.  (passive)","options":[],"answer":"Arabic is spoken in more than twenty countries.","explanation":"Include or omit by + agent.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل مع by."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite in the active to make the message clear and direct.","items":[{"q":"Your complaint will be answered within 24 hours. (We…)  (active)","options":[],"answer":"We will answer your complaint within 24 hours.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"The window was broken by Omar. (Omar…)  (active)","options":[],"answer":"Omar broke the window.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"It is guaranteed that the product will work. (We…)  (active)","options":[],"answer":"We guarantee that the product will work.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Analyze the voice.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence an ad might use with the passive to sound impressive.","options":[],"answer":"Recommended by nine out of ten dentists.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite it in the active and say what is missing.","options":[],"answer":"Nine out of ten dentists recommend it. – We still don't know which dentists or how many were asked.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write an honest apology in the active voice.","options":[],"answer":"We delivered your order late, and we apologize.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a news headline in the passive.","options":[],"answer":"New Hospital Opened in Taif","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a scientific sentence in the passive.","options":[],"answer":"The plants were watered every morning for two weeks.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Ad detective.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 passive sentences that hide information in an ad.","options":[],"answer":"Prices have been reduced. The formula has been improved.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 questions a smart consumer should ask about them.","options":[],"answer":"Reduced from what price? Improved how, and by whom?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite one sentence in the active with real information.","options":[],"answer":"We reduced the price from 300 to 250 riyals.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Tic-Tac-Grammar","mechanic":"tic_tac_toe","rules":["Two teams: X and O. Team X chooses a square.","Using the cue, the team says a correct sentence with choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent (the form in brackets: +, – or ?).","If the class and teacher agree it is correct, the team marks the square.","Three squares in a row wins. Play a second round with the grid turned around."],"data":[{"cue":"you / not / bring / your own food (don't have to)"},{"cue":"visitors / not / take photos (mustn't)"},{"cue":"I / buy / so many snacks / we had plenty (needn't have)"},{"cue":"the parcel / arrive / yesterday / it didn't (was supposed to)"},{"cue":"ads / be / honest (are supposed to)"},{"cue":"the product / test / by scientists (has been)"},{"cue":"mistakes / make / in the ad (were made)"},{"cue":"we / pay / extra / the exchange was free (didn't have to)"},{"cue":"consumers / read / the small print (need to)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which sentence takes the most responsibility?","options":["I lost your book, and I'll buy you a new one.","The book was lost somehow.","Your book has gone missing.","Your book was lost."],"answer":"I lost your book, and I'll buy you a new one.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"Which sentence is best for a museum sign about an old building?","options":["The mosque built in 1792 by people.","People built the mosque in 1792 by someone.","Someone built the mosque in 1792.","The mosque was built in 1792."],"answer":"The mosque was built in 1792.","explanation":"Passive voice to focus on the result.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"","options":["The results were analyze by a team of independent scientists.","The results were analyzing by a team of independent scientists.","The results were analyzed by a team of independent scientists.","The results analyzed by a team of independent scientists."],"answer":"The results were analyzed by a team of independent scientists.","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"Why might a company write \"Prices have been increased\" instead of \"We have increased prices\"?","options":["to sound more personal","to avoid taking responsibility","to make a promise","to name the person responsible"],"answer":"to avoid taking responsibility","explanation":"Why did the writer choose this voice?","explanation_ar":"سبب الاختيار."},{"q":"In which sentence is \"by + agent\" unnecessary?","options":["The painting was created by a 12-year-old student.","The novel was written by Naguib Mahfouz.","The theory was proposed by Ibn al-Haytham.","The thief was arrested by the police."],"answer":"The thief was arrested by the police.","explanation":"Include or omit by + agent.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل مع by."},{"q":"","options":["Arabic coffee usually is serving with dates.","Arabic coffee is usually served with dates.","Arabic coffee usually serves with dates.","Arabic coffee is usually serve with dates."],"answer":"Arabic coffee is usually served with dates.","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"A coach wants to praise his players directly. Which sentence is best?","options":["It was played well.","Brilliant playing was seen today.","You played brilliantly today!","The game was played brilliantly."],"answer":"You played brilliantly today!","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"The Kaaba is covered each year with a new kiswah, a black cloth that ______ with verses of the Quran in gold thread.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["is embroidering","embroiders","is embroidered","embroidered by"],"answer":"is embroidered","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"Penicillin, one of the most important medicines in history, ______ by Alexander Fleming in 1928.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["discovered","has discovering","was discover","was discovered"],"answer":"was discovered","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"\"Over 1,000 customers have been helped.\" What information is missing?","options":["who helped them","when it is now","whether customers were helped","how many customers there were"],"answer":"who helped them","explanation":"Why did the writer choose this voice?","explanation_ar":"سبب الاختيار."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"Two posters side by side: one says \"Mistakes were made. Prices have been increased.\" (passive, grey and vague) and the other says \"We made a mistake. We're sorry, and we'll fix it.\" (active, bright and clear); Lina and Omar comparing them with question marks. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two posters side by side: one says \"Mistakes were made. Prices have been increased.\" (passive, grey and vague) and the other says \"We made a mistake. We're sorry, and we'll fix it.\" (active, bright and clear); Lina and Omar comparing them with question marks"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Persuasion and Advertising”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows endorse (to say publicly that you support a product or a person); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing endorse: to say publicly that you support a product or a person"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows misleading (giving a wrong idea or impression); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing misleading: giving a wrong idea or impression"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows guarantee (a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing guarantee: a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows genuine (real, not fake); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing genuine: real, not fake"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Tic-Tac-Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Tic-Tac-Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W01","script":"endorse. endorse. A famous athlete endorses this brand of shoes.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W02","script":"misleading. misleading. The photo in the ad was misleading; the room was much smaller.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W03","script":"guarantee. guarantee. The laptop comes with a two-year guarantee.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-W04","script":"genuine. genuine. Is this a genuine leather bag?","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “\"Mistakes Were Made\": Active vs Passive for Effect”. Let’s learn choosing active or passive voice for effect: passive to focus on the result or the object, to hide or omit the agent, and to sound formal or scientific; active for clear responsibility, energy and directness; including or omitting by + agent together. Remember these patterns: Active: The company raised prices. • Passive: Prices were raised. Active: Nobody tested the cream. • Passive: The cream wasn't tested. Active: Who designed the logo? • Passive: Who was the logo designed by? The active voice says who does the action. It is clear, direct and energetic: We guarantee every product. Our team designed this app. Writers use it when they want to show responsibility or make a strong, personal message. The passive voice focuses on the action or the result, not the doer: Your order has been shipped. Writers choose it when the agent is unknown, obvious or unimportant (The road was repaired last week), when they want to sound formal or scientific (The samples were heated to 50°C), or when they want to hide responsibility (Mistakes were made. – by whom?). Here are some examples. Your package has been delivered. (focus on the package) Our engineers tested the phone in extreme heat. (credit to the engineers) Penicillin was discovered in 1928. Be careful! Some learners say: “The cake was eaten by someone. (we know it was Omar).” That’s not right. We say: “Omar ate the cake.” Agent known and important → active. Quick check: \"Mistakes were made.\" Do we know who made the mistakes? … Think! … No – the passive hides the doer. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain why an ad says \"Clinically tested\" instead of \"We tested it.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3","title":"Cheap or Affordable? Persuasive Language and Connotation","objectives":["Students will be able to explain how words with similar meanings have different connotations.","Students will be able to identify persuasive techniques and loaded words in advertisements.","I can explain why an ad says \"affordable\" instead of \"cheap\".","I can name persuasive techniques like bandwagon and testimonial."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من شرح كيف تختلف الدلالات الإيحائية لكلمات متقاربة المعنى.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التعرف على أساليب الإقناع والكلمات المشحونة في الإعلانات.","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا يقول الإعلان affordable بدلًا من cheap.","أستطيع أن أسمّي أساليب الإقناع مثل bandwagon وtestimonial."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"connotation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɑnəˈteɪʃən/","definition":"the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning","arabic":"الدلالة الإيحائية","example":"\"Slim\" has a more positive connotation than \"skinny\".","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W01"},{"word":"exaggerate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzædʒɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is","arabic":"يبالغ","example":"The ad exaggerates how fast the car is.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W02"},{"word":"slogan","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsloʊɡən/","definition":"a short, memorable phrase used in advertising","arabic":"شعار","example":"The company's slogan is \"Think Bigger.\"","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W03"},{"word":"target audience","pos":"noun phrase","ipa":"/ˈtɑrɡət ˈɑdiəns/","definition":"the group of people an advertisement is aimed at","arabic":"الجمهور المستهدف","example":"The target audience for this game is teenagers.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W04"},{"word":"bias","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbaɪəs/","definition":"an unfair preference for or against someone or something","arabic":"تحيّز","example":"The review showed a clear bias toward one brand.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W05"},{"word":"brand","pos":"noun","ipa":"/brænd/","definition":"the name of a product made by a particular company","arabic":"علامة تجارية","example":"Which brand of sports shoes do you prefer?","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W06"},{"word":"affordable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/əˈfɔrdəbəl/","definition":"not too expensive; cheap enough for people to buy","arabic":"ميسور التكلفة","example":"The company makes affordable laptops for students.","image_id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Denotation is a word's dictionary meaning. Connotation is the feeling it suggests. Words with similar denotations can have positive, neutral or negative connotations: affordable (+) / inexpensive (neutral) / cheap (–, may suggest low quality); slim (+) / thin (neutral) / skinny (–); determined (+) / stubborn (–); aroma (+) / smell (neutral) / odor (–); vintage (+) / old / outdated (–).\nAdvertisers choose loaded words with strong positive connotations (fresh, natural, genuine, premium, exclusive) and use persuasive techniques: bandwagon (everyone is buying it), testimonial (a famous person endorses it), emotional appeal (makes you feel happy, scared or guilty), exaggeration (the best in the world), scarcity (only 3 left! offer ends today!).\nA smart consumer asks: What is the claim? Is there evidence? Who is the target audience? Is there bias? In SAT Words in Context questions, choose the word whose meaning and connotation fit the passage exactly.\nCollocations/phrases: cheap → inexpensive → affordable • skinny → thin → slim | stubborn → firm → determined • nosy → curious → inquisitive | odor → smell → aroma • outdated → old → vintage/classic | bossy → assertive → confident leader • weird → unusual → unique","examples":["The brand's slogan is short and memorable.","The target audience for this game is teenagers aged 13–17.","A famous athlete endorses the shoes – that's a testimonial.","\"Join millions of happy users!\" is a bandwagon appeal.","The ad exaggerates; no drink can make you \"unstoppable\".","The photo was misleading because the burger was much smaller in real life.","The store sells affordable furniture for students.","The review seemed to have a bias toward one brand."],"common_errors":["✗ Our restaurant serves cheap meals! (in an ad) → ✓ Our restaurant serves affordable meals! (cheap can suggest low quality.)","✗ She is very stubborn to succeed. (praise) → ✓ She is very determined to succeed. (Positive connotation → determined.)","✗ The ad made a claim that it's the best, so it must be true. → ✓ The ad made a claim, but where is the evidence? (A claim is not proof.)","✗ The bakery has a wonderful odor. → ✓ The bakery has a wonderful aroma. (odor usually sounds unpleasant.)"],"arabic":"المعنى المعجمي (denotation) هو معنى الكلمة في القاموس، والدلالة الإيحائية (connotation) هي الشعور الذي توحي به.\nكلمات متقاربة المعنى مختلفة الإيحاء: affordable (إيجابي) / inexpensive (محايد) / cheap (سلبي قد يوحي برداءة الجودة).\nأساليب الإقناع: bandwagon (الجميع يشتريه)، testimonial (شهادة مشهور)، emotional appeal (إثارة المشاعر)، exaggeration (المبالغة)، scarcity (الندرة: بقي ٣ فقط!).\nفي أسئلة Words in Context في SAT اختر الكلمة التي يناسب معناها وإيحاؤها النص تمامًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Cheap or Affordable? Persuasive Language and Connotation”. Let’s learn denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U4-LS3 | Cheap or Affordable? Persuasive Language and Connotation","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. connotation: the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning. exaggerate: to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is. slogan: a short, memorable phrase used in advertising.","on_screen":"connotation | exaggerate | slogan","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Denotation is a word's dictionary meaning. Connotation is the feeling it suggests. Words with similar denotations can have positive, neutral or negative connotations: affordable (+) / inexpensive (neutral) / cheap (–, may suggest low quality); slim (+) / thin (neutral) / skinny (–); determined (+) / stubborn (–); aroma (+) / smell (neutral) / odor (–); vintage (+) / old / outdated (–). Advertisers choose loaded words with strong positive connotations (fresh, natural, genuine, premium, exclusive) and use persuasive techniques: bandwagon (everyone is buying it), testimonial (a famous person endorses it), emotional appeal (makes you feel happy, scared or guilty), exaggeration (the best in the world), scarcity (only 3 left! offer ends today!).","on_screen":"denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The brand's slogan is short and memorable. The target audience for this game is teenagers aged 13–17. A famous athlete endorses the shoes – that's a testimonial.","on_screen":"The brand's slogan is short and memorable. / The target audience for this game is teenagers aged 13–17. / A famous athlete endorses the shoes – that's a testimonial.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Our restaurant serves cheap meals! (in an ad).” That’s not right. We say: “Our restaurant serves affordable meals!.” cheap can suggest low quality.","on_screen":"✗ Our restaurant serves cheap meals! (in an ad)   ✓ Our restaurant serves affordable meals!","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: Which word sounds more positive: \"cheap\" or \"affordable\"? … Think! … *affordable*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain why an ad says \"affordable\" instead of \"cheap\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain why an ad says \"affordable\" instead of \"cheap\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","المعنى المعجمي (denotation) هو معنى الكلمة في القاموس، والدلالة الإيحائية (connotation) هي الشعور الذي توحي به.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Our restaurant serves cheap meals! (in an ad) — الصحيح: Our restaurant serves affordable meals!","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا يقول الإعلان affordable بدلًا من cheap."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"A restaurant wants its prices to sound good. Which word should it use?","options":["affordable","bargain-basement","cheap","low-quality"],"answer":"affordable","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"\"Join over five million happy customers!\" Which technique is this?","options":["scarcity","bandwagon","testimonial","exaggeration"],"answer":"bandwagon","explanation":"Name the persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"A famous footballer says, \"I only wear these boots.\" Which technique is this?","options":["testimonial","statistics","scarcity","bandwagon"],"answer":"testimonial","explanation":"Name the persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"Which word has the most negative connotation?","options":["thin","slender","slim","skinny"],"answer":"skinny","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"The photo in the ad was ___; the hotel room was half the size in real life.","options":["genuine","misleading","endorsed","affordable"],"answer":"misleading","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"Although the critic did not enjoy the film, she praised the director as ______ rather than stubborn, explaining that he had refused to give up on his vision despite years of rejection.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["indifferent","careless","determined","arrogant"],"answer":"determined","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the technique to the example.","items":[{"q":"scarcity","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","explanation":"scarcity → \"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\". Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"emotional appeal","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","explanation":"emotional appeal → a sad, hungry child in a charity ad. Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"exaggeration","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","explanation":"exaggeration → \"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\". Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"testimonial","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","explanation":"testimonial → \"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\". Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"bandwagon","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","explanation":"bandwagon → \"Everyone at school already has one!\". Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"slogan","options":["\"I'm a champion swimmer, and I drink AquaMax.\"","\"The most delicious pizza in the universe!\"","\"Hurry! Only three left in stock!\"","\"Everyone at school already has one!\"","a sad, hungry child in a charity ad","\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\""],"answer":"\"Think Bigger. Go Further.\"","explanation":"slogan → \"Think Bigger. Go Further.\". Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Think like an advertiser – and a consumer.","items":[{"q":"Write a positive and a negative word for \"a small apartment\".","options":[],"answer":"cozy (positive) / cramped (negative)","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a slogan for a reusable water bottle.","options":[],"answer":"Drink Smart. Waste Nothing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Who is the target audience for a new video game?","options":[],"answer":"The target audience is teenagers who like action games.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"exaggerate\".","options":[],"answer":"The ad exaggerates how long the battery lasts.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What question should a consumer ask about the claim \"Doctors recommend it\"?","options":[],"answer":"Which doctors, and how many of them?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Create and analyze an ad.","items":[{"q":"Write a 3-line advertisement for a school snack, using two positive loaded words.","options":[],"answer":"Try our fresh, natural date bars! Full of energy for busy students. Only 5 riyals!","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Name the persuasive techniques you used.","options":[],"answer":"Loaded words (fresh, natural) and emotional appeal (energy for busy students).","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one honest sentence that gives evidence.","options":[],"answer":"Each bar contains 6 grams of protein and no added sugar.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Memory Match","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Place all cards face down in a grid.","Turn over two cards. If the word matches its definition, keep the pair and say a sentence with the word.","If not, turn them back over; the next player plays.","The player with the most pairs wins."],"data":[{"word":"advertisement","match":"a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something","arabic":"إعلان"},{"word":"consumer","match":"a person who buys goods or services","arabic":"مستهلك"},{"word":"claim","match":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء"},{"word":"persuade","match":"to make someone agree to do or believe something","arabic":"يُقنع"},{"word":"endorse","match":"to say publicly that you support a product or a person","arabic":"يروّج لـ / يؤيّد"},{"word":"misleading","match":"giving a wrong idea or impression","arabic":"مضلِّل"},{"word":"guarantee","match":"a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem","arabic":"ضمان"},{"word":"genuine","match":"real, not fake","arabic":"أصلي / حقيقي"},{"word":"connotation","match":"the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning","arabic":"الدلالة الإيحائية"},{"word":"exaggerate","match":"to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is","arabic":"يبالغ"},{"word":"slogan","match":"a short, memorable phrase used in advertising","arabic":"شعار"},{"word":"target audience","match":"the group of people an advertisement is aimed at","arabic":"الجمهور المستهدف"},{"word":"bias","match":"an unfair preference for or against someone or something","arabic":"تحيّز"},{"word":"brand","match":"the name of a product made by a particular company","arabic":"علامة تجارية"},{"word":"affordable","match":"not too expensive; cheap enough for people to buy","arabic":"ميسور التكلفة"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word would a perfume ad use?","options":["smell bad","stench","odor","aroma"],"answer":"aroma","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"A friend who always asks about other people's private business is ___.","options":["interested","curious","nosy","inquisitive"],"answer":"nosy","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"\"Offer ends at midnight!\" uses the technique of ___.","options":["testimonial","statistics","scarcity","bandwagon"],"answer":"scarcity","explanation":"Name the persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"A company that pays a celebrity to say good things about its product wants her to ___ it.","options":["target","endorse","exaggerate","mislead"],"answer":"endorse","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"The ___ for this toy is children aged five to eight.","options":["target audience","bias","guarantee","slogan"],"answer":"target audience","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"The phone comes with a two-year ___, so they will repair it for free.","options":["slogan","claim","guarantee","bias"],"answer":"guarantee","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"Is this a ___ diamond or a fake one?","options":["affordable","biased","misleading","genuine"],"answer":"genuine","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"Which word has the most positive connotation?","options":["arrogant","bossy","proud of himself","confident"],"answer":"confident","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"The reviewer had worked for the company for ten years, so many readers suspected that his glowing review showed a clear ______ in its favor.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["connotation","bias","guarantee","slogan"],"answer":"bias","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Instead of describing the old house as \"outdated,\" the real estate agent called its wooden doors and stone floors ______, hoping buyers would see them as charming and valuable.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["classic","dull","worn-out","broken"],"answer":"classic","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A wall of colorful advertisements: a footballer holding a drink (testimonial), crowds wearing the same shoes with \"Everyone's wearing it!\" (bandwagon), a sad puppy for a charity (emotional appeal), \"The BEST pizza in the UNIVERSE!\" (exaggeration) and \"Only 2 left!\" (scarcity); a word-connotation thermometer from negative to positive. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A wall of colorful advertisements: a footballer holding a drink (testimonial), crowds wearing the same shoes with \"Everyone's wearing it!\" (bandwagon), a sad puppy for a charity (emotional appeal), \"The BEST pizza in the UNIVERSE!\" (exaggeration) and \"Only 2 left!\" (scarcity); a word-connotation thermometer from negative to positive"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows connotation (the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing connotation: the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows exaggerate (to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing exaggerate: to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows slogan (a short, memorable phrase used in advertising); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing slogan: a short, memorable phrase used in advertising"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows target audience (the group of people an advertisement is aimed at); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing target audience: the group of people an advertisement is aimed at"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows bias (an unfair preference for or against someone or something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing bias: an unfair preference for or against someone or something"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows brand (the name of a product made by a particular company); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing brand: the name of a product made by a particular company"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows affordable (not too expensive; cheap enough for people to buy); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing affordable: not too expensive; cheap enough for people to buy"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Memory Match”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Memory Match"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W01","script":"connotation. connotation. \"Slim\" has a more positive connotation than \"skinny\".","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W02","script":"exaggerate. exaggerate. The ad exaggerates how fast the car is.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W03","script":"slogan. slogan. The company's slogan is \"Think Bigger.\"","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W04","script":"target audience. target audience. The target audience for this game is teenagers.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W05","script":"bias. bias. The review showed a clear bias toward one brand.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W06","script":"brand. brand. Which brand of sports shoes do you prefer?","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-W07","script":"affordable. affordable. The company makes affordable laptops for students.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Cheap or Affordable? Persuasive Language and Connotation”. Let’s learn denotation and connotation (positive, neutral, negative: cheap / affordable / inexpensive; slim / skinny; determined / stubborn); persuasive techniques in advertising (bandwagon, testimonial, emotional appeal, exaggeration, scarcity); advertising vocabulary together. Meet some new words. connotation: the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning. exaggerate: to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is. slogan: a short, memorable phrase used in advertising. Denotation is a word's dictionary meaning. Connotation is the feeling it suggests. Words with similar denotations can have positive, neutral or negative connotations: affordable (+) / inexpensive (neutral) / cheap (–, may suggest low quality); slim (+) / thin (neutral) / skinny (–); determined (+) / stubborn (–); aroma (+) / smell (neutral) / odor (–); vintage (+) / old / outdated (–). Advertisers choose loaded words with strong positive connotations (fresh, natural, genuine, premium, exclusive) and use persuasive techniques: bandwagon (everyone is buying it), testimonial (a famous person endorses it), emotional appeal (makes you feel happy, scared or guilty), exaggeration (the best in the world), scarcity (only 3 left! offer ends today!). Here are some examples. The brand's slogan is short and memorable. The target audience for this game is teenagers aged 13–17. A famous athlete endorses the shoes – that's a testimonial. Be careful! Some learners say: “Our restaurant serves cheap meals! (in an ad).” That’s not right. We say: “Our restaurant serves affordable meals!.” cheap can suggest low quality. Quick check: Which word sounds more positive: \"cheap\" or \"affordable\"? … Think! … *affordable*. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain why an ad says \"affordable\" instead of \"cheap\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4","title":"The Company Changed Its Logo: Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement and Vague Pronouns","objectives":["Students will be able to make pronouns agree with their antecedents in number and person.","Students will be able to find and fix vague pronouns so that the reader knows exactly who or what is meant.","I can write \"The company changed its logo\" (not \"their logo\").","I can fix \"When Saad met Faisal, he was nervous.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من جعل الضمائر تتفق مع الأسماء التي تعود عليها في العدد والشخص.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اكتشاف الضمائر الغامضة وتصحيحها ليعرف القارئ تمامًا مَن أو ماذا يقصد.","أستطيع أن أكتب The company changed its logo (وليس their logo).","أستطيع أن أصحّح When Saad met Faisal, he was nervous."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"advertisement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ædˈvɝtəzmənt/","definition":"a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something","arabic":"إعلان","example":"I saw an advertisement for a new phone.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"consumer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsumɚ/","definition":"a person who buys goods or services","arabic":"مستهلك","example":"Consumers have the right to honest information.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"claim","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kleɪm/","definition":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء","example":"The ad made the claim that the cream works in one day.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"persuade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pɚˈsweɪd/","definition":"to make someone agree to do or believe something","arabic":"يُقنع","example":"The salesman persuaded my father to buy a bigger TV.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"endorse","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛnˈdɔrs/","definition":"to say publicly that you support a product or a person","arabic":"يروّج لـ / يؤيّد","example":"A famous athlete endorses this brand of shoes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"misleading","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɪˈslidɪŋ/","definition":"giving a wrong idea or impression","arabic":"مضلِّل","example":"The photo in the ad was misleading; the room was much smaller.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number. A company, a brand, a team or a government is singular in American English, so use it / its: The company changed its logo. Plural nouns take they / their: The customers shared their reviews.\nDon't shift person in a sentence or paragraph: (not: If you compare prices, one will save money.) → If you compare prices, you will save money. Choose you, we or one and stay with it.\nA vague pronoun doesn't clearly refer to one noun. (not: When Saad met Faisal, he was nervous.) (Who?) → When Saad met Faisal, Saad was nervous. (not: They say this drink is healthy.) (Who are they?) → Nutritionists say… (not: The ad promised free delivery and a gift, but this was false.) → … but this promise was false. Also use the right case: Lina and I (subject) went; the gift was for Lina and me (object).\ncompany / brand / team → it, its • plural noun → they, their\ndon't shift between you / one / we\nvague he / she / they / it / this → name the noun\nsubject: Lina and I • object: Lina and me","examples":["The brand launched its new phone in Riyadh.","The shoppers compared their receipts.","The team celebrated its victory.","If you read the small print, you will avoid surprises.","When Omar called Adam, Omar apologized.","Experts say that the cream has no effect.","The ad promised free delivery, but this promise was false.","The manager gave the vouchers to Lina and me."],"common_errors":["✗ The company announced their new prices. → ✓ The company announced its new prices. (Company → singular → its.)","✗ If you want a refund, one must keep the receipt. → ✓ If you want a refund, you must keep the receipt. (Don't shift person.)","✗ When the manager spoke to the customer, she was angry. → ✓ When the manager spoke to the customer, the customer was angry. (Vague she → name the person.)","✗ Me and Saad saw the ad. → ✓ Saad and I saw the ad. (Subject → I.)"],"arabic":"الضمير يتفق مع الاسم الذي يعود عليه في العدد: The company changed its logo (الشركة مفرد ← its)، والجمع ← their.\nلا نغيّر الضمير بين you وone وwe في الجملة نفسها.\nالضمير الغامض لا يُعرف على مَن يعود: When Saad met Faisal, he was nervous (مَن المتوتر؟)، فنذكر الاسم.\nthey بلا مرجع (They say…) وthis بلا اسم واضح ضمائر غامضة أيضًا. وهذه المهارة تُختبر في SAT ضمن Form, Structure, and Sense."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Company Changed Its Logo: Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement and Vague Pronouns”. Let’s learn pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U4-LS4 | The Company Changed Its Logo: Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement and Vague Pronouns","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number. A company, a brand, a team or a government is singular in American English, so use it / its: The company changed its logo. Plural nouns take they / their: The customers shared their reviews.","on_screen":"pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number. A company, a brand, a team or a government is singular in American English, so use it / its: The company changed its logo. Plural nouns take they / their: The customers shared their reviews. Don't shift person in a sentence or paragraph: (not: If you compare prices, one will save money.) → If you compare prices, you will save money. Choose you, we or one and stay with it.","on_screen":"company / brand / team → it, its • plural noun → they, their • don't shift between you / one / we • vague he / she / they / it / this → name the noun","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The brand launched its new phone in Riyadh. The shoppers compared their receipts. The team celebrated its victory.","on_screen":"The brand launched its new phone in Riyadh. / The shoppers compared their receipts. / The team celebrated its victory.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The company announced their new prices.” That’s not right. We say: “The company announced its new prices.” Company → singular → its.","on_screen":"✗ The company announced their new prices.   ✓ The company announced its new prices.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"The company changed their logo.\" What's wrong? … Think! … *company* is singular → *its*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The company changed its logo\" (not \"their logo\").","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The company changed its logo\" (not \"their logo\").","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الضمير يتفق مع الاسم الذي يعود عليه في العدد: The company changed its logo (الشركة مفرد ← its)، والجمع ← their.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The company announced their new prices. — الصحيح: The company announced its new prices.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The company changed its logo (وليس their logo)."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"My Review of a Phone Shop (editing passage)","body":"Last week, me and my brother visited a new phone shop in the mall. The shop had just opened, and their prices were very affordable. When my brother talked to the salesman, he offered him a discount. They were friendly and patient. The shop gave free screen protectors to my brother and I. If you need a phone, one should visit it.","word_count":62,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The company changed ___ logo last year.","options":["its","it's","their","they're"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"The customers left ___ reviews on the website.","options":["its","it's","their","his"],"answer":"their","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"If you compare prices, ___ will save money.","options":["one","you","they","we"],"answer":"you","explanation":"No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"لا تغيير في الشخص."},{"q":"","options":["Saad and me watched the advertisement together.","Saad and I watched the advertisement together.","Me and Saad watched the advertisement together.","I and Saad watched the advertisement together."],"answer":"Saad and I watched the advertisement together.","explanation":"Subject or object pronoun.","explanation_ar":"حالة الضمير."},{"q":"Which sentence has a vague pronoun?","options":["When Omar called Adam, Omar apologized.","Adam received an apology from Omar.","Omar apologized when he called Adam.","When Omar called Adam, he apologized."],"answer":"When Omar called Adam, he apologized.","explanation":"Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"In 2021, the Saudi coffee brand expanded ______ business to five new countries, opening cafés in Cairo, Amman and Dubai.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["their","they're","it's","its"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Write the correct pronoun. Word bank: its, you, me","items":[{"q":"The team celebrated ______ victory with the fans.","options":[],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"The free gifts were for my brother and ______.","options":[],"answer":"me","explanation":"Subject or object pronoun.","explanation_ar":"حالة الضمير."},{"q":"If you want a refund, ______ must keep the receipt.","options":[],"answer":"you","explanation":"No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"لا تغيير في الشخص."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The brand launched their new phone in Riyadh.","options":[],"answer":"The brand launched its new phone in Riyadh.","explanation":"their → its. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: their → its. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"They say that this drink has too much sugar.","options":[],"answer":"Nutritionists say that this drink has too much sugar.","explanation":"They say → Nutritionists say. Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: They say → Nutritionists say. الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"If you read the terms, one will understand your rights.","options":[],"answer":"If you read the terms, you will understand your rights.","explanation":"one → you. No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: one → you. لا تغيير في الشخص."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the review.","items":[{"q":"Last week, me and my brother visited a new phone shop in the mall.","options":[],"answer":"Last week, I and my brother visited a new phone shop in the mall.","explanation":"me → I. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: me → I. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"The shop had just opened, and their prices were very affordable.","options":[],"answer":"The shop had just opened, and its prices were very affordable.","explanation":"their → its. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: their → its. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"When my brother talked to the salesman, he offered him a discount. The staff were friendly and patient.","options":[],"answer":"When my brother talked to the salesman, the salesman offered him a discount. The staff were friendly and patient.","explanation":"he → the salesman. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: he → the salesman. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"When my brother talked to the salesman, the salesman offered him a discount. They were friendly and patient.","options":[],"answer":"When my brother talked to the salesman, the salesman offered him a discount. The staff were friendly and patient.","explanation":"They → The staff. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: They → The staff. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"The shop gave free screen protectors to my brother and I.","options":[],"answer":"The shop gave free screen protectors to my brother and me.","explanation":"I → me. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: I → me. الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"If you need a phone, one should visit it.","options":[],"answer":"If you need a phone, you should visit it.","explanation":"one → you. Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: one → you. الاتفاق في العدد."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write clear sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence about a company and its product.","options":[],"answer":"The company improved its battery technology.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Fix this vague sentence: \"When Lina showed Sara the ad, she laughed.\"","options":[],"answer":"When Lina showed Sara the ad, Sara laughed.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"my friend and me\" used correctly.","options":[],"answer":"The coach gave the tickets to my friend and me.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Builder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The teacher reads a word and a short clue.","Teams build the word with letter tiles in 30 seconds.","The team holds up the word; correct spelling = 1 point, with correct capital letter or punctuation where needed = 1 bonus point.","Continue until all words are used."],"data":[{"word":"advertisement","clue":"a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something"},{"word":"consumer","clue":"a person who buys goods or services"},{"word":"guarantee","clue":"a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem"},{"word":"genuine","clue":"real, not fake"},{"word":"exaggerate","clue":"to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is"},{"word":"misleading","clue":"giving a wrong idea or impression"},{"word":"claim","clue":"a statement that something is true, often without proof"},{"word":"persuade","clue":"to make someone agree to do or believe something"},{"word":"endorse","clue":"to say publicly that you support a product or a person"},{"word":"connotation","clue":"the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The government announced ___ new consumer protection law.","options":["its","their","there","it's"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"Shoppers should always keep ___ receipts.","options":["it's","their","its","his"],"answer":"their","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"","options":["If we read the labels carefully, you can make healthier choices.","If we read the labels carefully, one can make healthier choices.","If we read the labels carefully, they can make healthier choices.","If we read the labels carefully, we can make healthier choices."],"answer":"If we read the labels carefully, we can make healthier choices.","explanation":"No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"لا تغيير في الشخص."},{"q":"","options":["The salesman showed Faisal and I the new models.","The salesman showed I and Faisal the new models.","The salesman showed Faisal and me the new models.","The salesman showed me and Faisal and I the new models."],"answer":"The salesman showed Faisal and me the new models.","explanation":"Subject or object pronoun.","explanation_ar":"حالة الضمير."},{"q":"How can we fix \"When the coach spoke to the player, he was angry.\"?","options":["When the coach spoke to the player, the coach was angry.","When the coach spoke to the player, he were angry.","When he spoke to the player, he was angry.","When the coach spoke to him, he was angry."],"answer":"When the coach spoke to the player, the coach was angry.","explanation":"Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"In which sentence is it unclear what \"this\" refers to?","options":["The ad promised fast delivery, which never happened.","The ad's promise of fast delivery was not true.","The ad promised fast delivery, but this promise was not true.","The ad promised a free gift and fast delivery, but this was not true."],"answer":"The ad promised a free gift and fast delivery, but this was not true.","explanation":"Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"The football club thanked ___ fans after the final.","options":["its","it's","their","they're"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"Founded in 1945, the Arab League held ______ first meeting in Cairo, where it is still based today.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["it's","its","they're","their"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"Many coffee farmers in the Jazan region grow ______ beans on steep mountain terraces, a method that has been used for centuries.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["its","their","it's","his"],"answer":"their","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"When customers read online reviews, ______ should check whether the reviewers actually bought the product.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["you","it","they","one"],"answer":"they","explanation":"No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"لا تغيير في الشخص."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Persuasion and Advertising” that shows pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Persuasion and Advertising” that shows pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects in action"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Builder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Builder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Company Changed Its Logo: Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement and Vague Pronouns”. Let’s learn pronoun–antecedent agreement in number (company → it/its; customers → they/their); shifts in pronoun person (you / one / we); vague pronouns (he/she with two possible antecedents, they with no antecedent, this/it without a clear noun); pronoun case in compound subjects and objects together. A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number. A company, a brand, a team or a government is singular in American English, so use it / its: The company changed its logo. Plural nouns take they / their: The customers shared their reviews. A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number. A company, a brand, a team or a government is singular in American English, so use it / its: The company changed its logo. Plural nouns take they / their: The customers shared their reviews. Don't shift person in a sentence or paragraph: (not: If you compare prices, one will save money.) → If you compare prices, you will save money. Choose you, we or one and stay with it. Here are some examples. The brand launched its new phone in Riyadh. The shoppers compared their receipts. The team celebrated its victory. Be careful! Some learners say: “The company announced their new prices.” That’s not right. We say: “The company announced its new prices.” Company → singular → its. Quick check: \"The company changed their logo.\" What's wrong? … Think! … *company* is singular → *its*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The company changed its logo\" (not \"their logo\").","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5","title":"Analyzing an Ad: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to analyze a real or invented advertisement in a group.","Students will be able to write an ad analysis with modals, purposeful voice choices, connotation and clear pronouns, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can explain how an advertisement tries to persuade people.","I can write a short analysis of an ad."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تحليل إعلان حقيقي أو متخيَّل ضمن مجموعة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة تحليل لإعلان بأفعال الإلزام واختيار مقصود للمعلوم والمجهول ودلالات إيحائية وضمائر واضحة، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أشرح كيف يحاول الإعلان إقناع الناس.","أستطيع أن أكتب تحليلًا قصيرًا لإعلان."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"advertisement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ædˈvɝtəzmənt/","definition":"a picture, video or text that tries to persuade people to buy something","arabic":"إعلان","example":"I saw an advertisement for a new phone.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"consumer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsumɚ/","definition":"a person who buys goods or services","arabic":"مستهلك","example":"Consumers have the right to honest information.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"claim","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kleɪm/","definition":"a statement that something is true, often without proof","arabic":"ادّعاء","example":"The ad made the claim that the cream works in one day.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"persuade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pɚˈsweɪd/","definition":"to make someone agree to do or believe something","arabic":"يُقنع","example":"The salesman persuaded my father to buy a bigger TV.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"endorse","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɛnˈdɔrs/","definition":"to say publicly that you support a product or a person","arabic":"يروّج لـ / يؤيّد","example":"A famous athlete endorses this brand of shoes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"misleading","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɪˈslidɪŋ/","definition":"giving a wrong idea or impression","arabic":"مضلِّل","example":"The photo in the ad was misleading; the room was much smaller.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"guarantee","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɡɛrənˈti/","definition":"a written promise to repair or replace a product if it has a problem","arabic":"ضمان","example":"The laptop comes with a two-year guarantee.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"genuine","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈdʒɛnjəwən/","definition":"real, not fake","arabic":"أصلي / حقيقي","example":"Is this a genuine leather bag?","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"connotation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɑnəˈteɪʃən/","definition":"the feeling or idea that a word suggests in addition to its basic meaning","arabic":"الدلالة الإيحائية","example":"\"Slim\" has a more positive connotation than \"skinny\".","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"exaggerate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzædʒɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to make something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is","arabic":"يبالغ","example":"The ad exaggerates how fast the car is.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"slogan","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsloʊɡən/","definition":"a short, memorable phrase used in advertising","arabic":"شعار","example":"The company's slogan is \"Think Bigger.\"","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"target audience","pos":"noun phrase","ipa":"/ˈtɑrɡət ˈɑdiəns/","definition":"the group of people an advertisement is aimed at","arabic":"الجمهور المستهدف","example":"The target audience for this game is teenagers.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"bias","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbaɪəs/","definition":"an unfair preference for or against someone or something","arabic":"تحيّز","example":"The review showed a clear bias toward one brand.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"brand","pos":"noun","ipa":"/brænd/","definition":"the name of a product made by a particular company","arabic":"علامة تجارية","example":"Which brand of sports shoes do you prefer?","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"affordable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/əˈfɔrdəbəl/","definition":"not too expensive; cheap enough for people to buy","arabic":"ميسور التكلفة","example":"The company makes affordable laptops for students.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 4: modals of obligation, necessity and expectation; active and passive for effect; connotation and persuasive techniques; and clear, correct pronouns.\nAn ad analysis describes the ad, identifies the target audience, names the techniques, questions the claims (Who? What evidence?) and gives an opinion. Use transitions to show your reasoning: however (contrast), therefore / as a result (result), for example (evidence), in addition (another point).\n✔ must / don't have to / needn't have / be supposed to\n✔ passive can hide the agent – ask \"by whom?\"\n✔ connotation, slogan, testimonial, bandwagon, scarcity\n✔ company → its • no vague they / this","examples":["The ad is supposed to show the product honestly.","Viewers don't have to believe every claim.","The drink is advertised as \"natural,\" but it contains artificial colors.","The company claims that its drink gives energy.","The word \"premium\" has a positive connotation.","The footballer's testimonial makes the drink seem cool.","For example, the ad shows only happy, athletic teenagers.","The company needn't have exaggerated; the product is good enough."],"common_errors":["✗ The company explains their product clearly. → ✓ The company explains its product clearly. (company → its.)","✗ It says in the ad that it's healthy. → ✓ The ad says that the drink is healthy. (Avoid vague it.)","✗ Consumers mustn't believe every ad, but they can if they want. → ✓ Consumers don't have to believe every ad. (Not necessary → don't have to.)"],"arabic":"نراجع أفعال الإلزام والضرورة والتوقّع، والمعلوم والمجهول للتأثير، والدلالات الإيحائية وأساليب الإقناع، والضمائر الواضحة.\nتحليل الإعلان يصف الإعلان ويحدد الجمهور المستهدف ويسمّي الأساليب ويناقش الادعاءات ويعطي رأيًا.\nاستخدم الروابط لإظهار تفكيرك: however للتضاد، وtherefore للنتيجة، وfor example للدليل، وin addition لنقطة أخرى."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Analyzing an Ad: Language in Use”. Let’s learn modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U4-LS5 | Analyzing an Ad: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Ad detectives: each group receives an invented ad (a soft drink, a phone, a sports shoe, a summer camp). Identify the target audience, two persuasive techniques, one passive sentence that hides information and one misleading claim. Then rewrite the ad honestly.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 4: modals of obligation, necessity and expectation; active and passive for effect; connotation and persuasive techniques; and clear, correct pronouns. An ad analysis describes the ad, identifies the target audience, names the techniques, questions the claims (Who? What evidence?) and gives an opinion. Use transitions to show your reasoning: however (contrast), therefore / as a result (result), for example (evidence), in addition (another point).","on_screen":"✔ must / don't have to / needn't have / be supposed to • ✔ passive can hide the agent – ask \"by whom?\" • ✔ connotation, slogan, testimonial, bandwagon, scarcity","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The ad is supposed to show the product honestly. Viewers don't have to believe every claim. The drink is advertised as \"natural,\" but it contains artificial colors.","on_screen":"The ad is supposed to show the product honestly. / Viewers don't have to believe every claim. / The drink is advertised as \"natural,\" but it contains artificial colors.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The company explains their product clearly.” That’s not right. We say: “The company explains its product clearly.” company → its.","on_screen":"✗ The company explains their product clearly.   ✓ The company explains its product clearly.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “It says in the ad that it's healthy.”? … Think! … It should be “The ad says that the drink is healthy.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain how an advertisement tries to persuade people.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain how an advertisement tries to persuade people.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع أفعال الإلزام والضرورة والتوقّع، والمعلوم والمجهول للتأثير، والدلالات الإيحائية وأساليب الإقناع، والضمائر الواضحة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The company explains their product clearly. — الصحيح: The company explains its product clearly.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح كيف يحاول الإعلان إقناع الناس."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Analyzing an Ad – Lina","body":"The advertisement for FreshFizz shows a famous footballer drinking a can of the drink after scoring a goal.\nIt is clearly aimed at teenagers who admire athletes, so its target audience is easy to identify.\nThe slogan, \"Feel unstoppable!\", is an emotional appeal, but it doesn't tell consumers what is actually in the drink.\nThe small print says that the drink \"has been tested,\" but it doesn't say who tested it or what the results were.\nConsumers are supposed to check the sugar content; however, the label is very hard to read.\nIn my opinion, the company needn't have hidden this information, because honest ads build trust.\nBefore you buy any product, you must look past the slogan and check the facts yourself.","word_count":123,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Lina's analysis. True or False?","items":[{"q":"The ad shows a famous footballer.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The target audience is elderly people.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The slogan explains what is in the drink.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The small print doesn't say who tested the drink.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The label is easy to read.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Lina thinks honest ads build trust.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Analyzing an Ad – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The ad is clearly aimed at teenagers, so ___ target audience is easy to identify.","options":["it's","its","they're","their"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agreement or clarity.","explanation_ar":"الضمائر."},{"q":"The slogan \"Feel unstoppable!\" is an example of ___.","options":["a statistic","a guarantee","scarcity","an emotional appeal"],"answer":"an emotional appeal","explanation":"Connotation or persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"الإيحاء وأسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"The small print says the drink \"has been tested.\" Why is this passive sentence weak?","options":["It doesn't say who tested it.","It is in the active voice.","It names too many scientists.","It gives exact results."],"answer":"It doesn't say who tested it.","explanation":"Active or passive for effect.","explanation_ar":"المعلوم والمجهول."},{"q":"Consumers ___ check the sugar content, but the label is hard to read.","options":["supposing to","are suppose to","are supposed to","supposed"],"answer":"are supposed to","explanation":"Modal of obligation, necessity or expectation.","explanation_ar":"أفعال الإلزام."},{"q":"The company ___ hidden the information; honesty would have built more trust.","options":["don't have to","mustn't have","needn't have","didn't need"],"answer":"needn't have","explanation":"Modal of obligation, necessity or expectation.","explanation_ar":"أفعال الإلزام."},{"q":"The footballer's endorsement makes the drink look exciting. ______ the ad gives no information about the drink's ingredients or health effects.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","Similarly,","For example,","However,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Ad detectives: write your notes.","items":[{"q":"The product and the target audience: ___","options":[],"answer":"A new sports shoe for teenage runners","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Two persuasive techniques you found: ___","options":[],"answer":"testimonial (an Olympic runner) and scarcity (\"limited edition\")","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A passive or vague claim, and the question you would ask: ___","options":[],"answer":"\"Designed for champions\" – designed by whom, and for which champions?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A word with a strong positive connotation in the ad: ___","options":[],"answer":"\"lightning-fast\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Your honest version of the slogan: ___","options":[],"answer":"Comfortable shoes for everyday running.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your ad analysis.","items":[{"q":"Plan: describe the ad, audience, two techniques, one weak claim, opinion.","options":[],"answer":"summer camp ad; parents of children 10–14; emotional appeal (happy kids), bandwagon (\"everyone's signing up\"); \"experienced staff\" – how experienced?; seems good but needs details","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your analysis in 8–9 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"The advertisement for Desert Explorers Summer Camp shows smiling children riding camels and climbing dunes. Its target audience is parents of children aged ten to fourteen. The ad uses an emotional appeal, because the happy faces make parents imagine their own children having fun. It also uses the bandwagon technique with the words \"Everyone's signing up!\" The ad says that the camp \"is run by experienced staff,\" but it doesn't explain how experienced they are. Parents are supposed to read the full terms; however, the link is hidden at the bottom of the page. In my opinion, the camp needn't have exaggerated, because the activities already look exciting. Before parents book, they must ask for clear information about safety.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: modals? passive claims questioned? connotation? pronouns clear and correct? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"The camp explains their activities\" to \"The camp explains its activities\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Quiz Show Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams take turns choosing a category and a cue word.","Grammar: make a sentence with modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review). Words: give the meaning. Spelling: spell it. Write It!: write a sentence with correct punctuation.","Correct answers score 10 points; teams may steal a question another team misses.","The highest score after 8 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"advertisement","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"consumer","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"claim","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"persuade","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"endorse","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"misleading","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"guarantee","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"genuine","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"connotation","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"exaggerate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"slogan","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"target audience","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"bias","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"brand","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"affordable","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Advertisers ___ make false claims about medicines; it's against the law.","options":["mustn't","don't have to","don't need to","needn't"],"answer":"mustn't","explanation":"Modal of obligation, necessity or expectation.","explanation_ar":"أفعال الإلزام."},{"q":"You ___ buy the most expensive brand; cheaper ones work just as well.","options":["can't","don't have to","aren't allowed to","mustn't"],"answer":"don't have to","explanation":"Modal of obligation, necessity or expectation.","explanation_ar":"أفعال الإلزام."},{"q":"Which sentence takes clear responsibility?","options":["We increased the price because our costs went up.","Prices were changed.","It has been decided to raise prices.","The price has been increased."],"answer":"We increased the price because our costs went up.","explanation":"Active or passive for effect.","explanation_ar":"المعلوم والمجهول."},{"q":"Which word would a clothing store use in an ad?","options":["worn-out","outdated","old-fashioned","vintage"],"answer":"vintage","explanation":"Connotation or persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"الإيحاء وأسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"","options":["The brand apologized to it's customers for the delay.","The brand apologized to they're customers for the delay.","The brand apologized to its customers for the delay.","The brand apologized to their customers for the delay."],"answer":"The brand apologized to its customers for the delay.","explanation":"Pronoun agreement or clarity.","explanation_ar":"الضمائر."},{"q":"The new phone is advertised as waterproof. ______ many users have reported that it stopped working after light rain.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Likewise,","Therefore,","For instance,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Some ads make claims that are hard to check. ______ an ad might say that a product is \"loved by millions\" without explaining how this was measured.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","In contrast,","For example,","Instead,"],"answer":"For example,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Many food ads target children.\n• Some countries limit food ads during children's TV programs.\n• These ads often promote snacks high in sugar.\n• Doctors link high sugar intake to health problems in children.\n\nThe student wants to explain why some countries limit food ads for children. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Some countries have programs for children on TV.","Because food ads aimed at children often promote sugary snacks that doctors link to health problems, some countries limit these ads during children's programs.","Doctors study health problems in children.","Many food ads target children."],"answer":"Because food ads aimed at children often promote sugary snacks that doctors link to health problems, some countries limit these ads during children's programs.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Influencers often promote products on social media.\n• In many countries, paid posts must be clearly labeled as ads.\n• Some influencers don't label their paid posts.\n• Followers may not realize that the influencer was paid.\n\nThe student wants to explain why labeling paid posts is important. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Some influencers have many followers.","In many countries, there are rules about social media.","Influencers often promote products on social media.","Labeling paid posts is important because, without a label, followers may not realize that an influencer was paid to promote a product."],"answer":"Labeling paid posts is important because, without a label, followers may not realize that an influencer was paid to promote a product.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for an ad analysis:","options":["They say it's natural, but this is not true.","The company claims it's drink is natural; however, it's label shows colors.","The company claims that its drink is \"natural\"; however, the label shows that it contains artificial colors.","The company claims that their drink is \"natural\", however, the label show artificial colors."],"answer":"The company claims that its drink is \"natural\"; however, the label shows that it contains artificial colors.","explanation":"Pronoun agreement or clarity.","explanation_ar":"الضمائر."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"Lina presenting an enlarged soft-drink advertisement on the class screen: a footballer drinking a can with the slogan \"Feel unstoppable!\", tiny small print at the bottom circled in red; classmates taking notes. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Lina presenting an enlarged soft-drink advertisement on the class screen: a footballer drinking a can with the slogan \"Feel unstoppable!\", tiny small print at the bottom circled in red; classmates taking notes"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Persuasion and Advertising”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Persuasion and Advertising”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L08-U4-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Quiz Show Challenge”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Quiz Show Challenge"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS5-T1","script":"The advertisement for FreshFizz shows a famous footballer drinking a can of the drink after scoring a goal. It is clearly aimed at teenagers who admire athletes, so its target audience is easy to identify. The slogan, \"Feel unstoppable!\", is an emotional appeal, but it doesn't tell consumers what is actually in the drink. The small print says that the drink \"has been tested,\" but it doesn't say who tested it or what the results were. Consumers are supposed to check the sugar content; however, the label is very hard to read. In my opinion, the company needn't have hidden this information, because honest ads build trust. Before you buy any product, you must look past the slogan and check the facts yourself.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U4-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Analyzing an Ad: Language in Use”. Let’s learn modals of obligation and expectation, active vs passive for effect, connotation and persuasive techniques, clear pronouns; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Ad detectives: each group receives an invented ad (a soft drink, a phone, a sports shoe, a summer camp). Identify the target audience, two persuasive techniques, one passive sentence that hides information and one misleading claim. Then rewrite the ad honestly. Today you use all of Unit 4: modals of obligation, necessity and expectation; active and passive for effect; connotation and persuasive techniques; and clear, correct pronouns. An ad analysis describes the ad, identifies the target audience, names the techniques, questions the claims (Who? What evidence?) and gives an opinion. Use transitions to show your reasoning: however (contrast), therefore / as a result (result), for example (evidence), in addition (another point). Here are some examples. The ad is supposed to show the product honestly. Viewers don't have to believe every claim. The drink is advertised as \"natural,\" but it contains artificial colors. Be careful! Some learners say: “The company explains their product clearly.” That’s not right. We say: “The company explains its product clearly.” company → its. Quick check: what is wrong with “It says in the ad that it's healthy.”? … Think! … It should be “The ad says that the drink is healthy.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain how an advertisement tries to persuade people.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"You ___ use your phone during the exam; it's against the rules.","options":["don't have to","don't need to","mustn't","needn't"],"answer":"mustn't","explanation":"Prohibited → mustn't / not allowed to.","explanation_ar":"المنع."},{"q":"Entry to the museum is free, so you ___ buy a ticket.","options":["mustn't","can't","don't have to","aren't allowed to"],"answer":"don't have to","explanation":"Not necessary → don't have to / don't need to / needn't.","explanation_ar":"عدم الضرورة."},{"q":"I took an umbrella, but it didn't rain. I ___ it.","options":["don't have to take","didn't need to take","mustn't take","needn't have taken"],"answer":"needn't have taken","explanation":"Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"فعلته دون حاجة."},{"q":"The train ___ leave at 8:00, but it left at 8:40.","options":["had supposed","must have to","supposes to","was supposed to"],"answer":"was supposed to","explanation":"Expected → be supposed to (often not done).","explanation_ar":"التوقّع."},{"q":"","options":["We needn't have queue because we had booked online.","We didn't need to queue because we had booked online.","We didn't needed to queue because we had booked online.","We mustn't queue because we had booked online."],"answer":"We didn't need to queue because we had booked online.","explanation":"Past necessity → had to / didn't have to / didn't need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة في الماضي."},{"q":"According to new regulations, all paid social media posts ______ clearly labeled as advertisements so that followers know when content has been sponsored.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["must being","must be","must to be","must have be"],"answer":"must be","explanation":"Necessary → must / have to / need to.","explanation_ar":"الضرورة."},{"q":"A shop wants to apologize honestly for a delivery error. Which sentence should it use?","options":["We sent you the wrong item, and we will replace it today.","Your item was sent incorrectly.","A mistake has been made with your order.","The wrong item was sent."],"answer":"We sent you the wrong item, and we will replace it today.","explanation":"Active voice for clarity and responsibility.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمعلوم."},{"q":"Which sentence is best for a science report?","options":["I stirred the solution for five minutes and it was fun.","The solution stirred itself for five minutes.","The solution was stirred for five minutes.","Somebody stirred the solution."],"answer":"The solution was stirred for five minutes.","explanation":"Passive voice to focus on the result.","explanation_ar":"المبني للمجهول."},{"q":"","options":["The new airport was designed by an international team of architects.","The new airport was designing by an international team of architects.","The new airport designed by an international team of architects.","The new airport was design by an international team of architects."],"answer":"The new airport was designed by an international team of architects.","explanation":"be + past participle in the right tense.","explanation_ar":"صيغة المجهول."},{"q":"Why might an ad say \"Recommended by experts\" instead of naming them?","options":["to give exact information","to sound convincing without giving details","to show who is responsible","to be more personal"],"answer":"to sound convincing without giving details","explanation":"Why did the writer choose this voice?","explanation_ar":"سبب الاختيار."},{"q":"Which word would be the most flattering way to describe a student who asks many questions?","options":["meddling","inquisitive","prying","nosy"],"answer":"inquisitive","explanation":"Choose the word with the right connotation.","explanation_ar":"الدلالة الإيحائية."},{"q":"\"Only five tickets left – book now!\" Which technique is this?","options":["bandwagon","statistics","scarcity","testimonial"],"answer":"scarcity","explanation":"Name the persuasive technique.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب الإقناع."},{"q":"The cream's packaging was ___; it showed results that the product couldn't achieve.","options":["affordable","genuine","biased","misleading"],"answer":"misleading","explanation":"Advertising vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الإعلان."},{"q":"Rather than calling the small apartment \"cramped,\" the advertisement described it as ______, suggesting a warm and comfortable space for a young family.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["narrow","cozy","tiny","crowded"],"answer":"cozy","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The supermarket chain lowered ___ prices during Ramadan.","options":["their","they're","it's","its"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"","options":["If you check the ingredients, they will avoid unhealthy snacks.","If you check the ingredients, one will avoid unhealthy snacks.","If you check the ingredients, you will avoid unhealthy snacks.","If one checks the ingredients, you will avoid unhealthy snacks."],"answer":"If you check the ingredients, you will avoid unhealthy snacks.","explanation":"No shift in person (you / one / we).","explanation_ar":"لا تغيير في الشخص."},{"q":"","options":["The manager gave free samples to Adam and me.","The manager gave free samples to I and Adam.","The manager gave free samples to Adam and I.","The manager gave free samples to me and Adam and I."],"answer":"The manager gave free samples to Adam and me.","explanation":"Subject or object pronoun.","explanation_ar":"حالة الضمير."},{"q":"Which sentence leaves the reader unsure who frowned?","options":["When Saad showed Ziyad the receipt, he frowned.","Saad frowned when he saw the receipt.","Ziyad frowned when Saad showed him the receipt.","When Saad showed Ziyad the receipt, Ziyad frowned."],"answer":"When Saad showed Ziyad the receipt, he frowned.","explanation":"Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"The international car company recalled thousands of vehicles last year after ______ engineers discovered a problem with the brakes.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["their","they're","its","it's"],"answer":"its","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"Many people believe that expensive products are always better. ______ a recent consumer study found that several affordable brands performed just as well as premium ones.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Therefore,","Similarly,","For example,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Saudi Arabia's consumer protection rules require clear prices.\n• Shops must display prices on products or shelves.\n• Consumers can report violations to the Ministry of Commerce.\n• The ministry can fine shops that break the rules.\n\nThe student wants to explain how consumers can help enforce the rules. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The Ministry of Commerce exists in Saudi Arabia.","Shops display prices on shelves.","Saudi Arabia has consumer protection rules.","Consumers can report shops that don't display clear prices to the Ministry of Commerce, which can fine shops that break the rules."],"answer":"Consumers can report shops that don't display clear prices to the Ministry of Commerce, which can fine shops that break the rules.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Which sentence avoids vague pronouns?","options":["The ad say the shoes was designed by champions, but this is unclear.","The ad says the shoes were \"designed by champions,\" but it never explains which champions designed them.","It says in the ad that champions designed it.","The ad says they were designed by champions, but they never explain it."],"answer":"The ad says the shoes were \"designed by champions,\" but it never explains which champions designed them.","explanation":"Fix the vague pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الضمير الغامض."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the brand changed their slogan, and they says it is more modern.\"","options":["the brand changed its slogan and says that it is more modern.","The brand changed their slogan and say it is more modern.","The brand changed it's slogan, and they says it is more modern.","The brand changed its slogan and says that the new one is more modern."],"answer":"The brand changed its slogan and says that the new one is more modern.","explanation":"Pronoun agrees in number (it/its vs they/their).","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق في العدد."},{"q":"\"I needn't have bought a new case.\" What does this mean?","options":["I bought a case, but it wasn't necessary.","I'm not allowed to buy a case.","I didn't buy a case.","I must buy a case."],"answer":"I bought a case, but it wasn't necessary.","explanation":"Did it, but it wasn't necessary → needn't have + past participle.","explanation_ar":"فعلته دون حاجة."}],"theme_ar":"الإقناع والإعلان","essential_question_ar":"كيف يحاول المعلنون إقناعنا، وكيف نفكّر بأنفسنا؟","can_do":"I can talk about rules and necessity with modals such as don't have to, needn't have and be supposed to, choose active or passive voice for effect, recognize persuasive language and connotation, and use pronouns clearly and correctly.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أتحدث عن القواعد والضرورة بأفعال مثل don't have to وneedn't have وbe supposed to، وأختار المبني للمعلوم أو المجهول لتحقيق أثر معيّن، وأتعرّف على لغة الإقناع والدلالات الإيحائية، وأستخدم الضمائر بوضوح وصحة."});
